WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.613
An intelligence product should be clear, concise, audience-aware, and decision-oriented.

00:00:03.613 --> 00:00:07.829
It might be an incident update, threat assessment, risk summary, executive brief,

00:00:07.829 --> 00:00:11.342
detection note, fraud analysis, vendor advisory summary, or operational recommendation.

00:00:11.342 --> 00:00:16.260
Whatever the format, the product should help someone understand what matters, why it matters,

00:00:16.260 --> 00:00:20.827
how confident the analyst is, and what decision or next step is supported.

00:00:20.827 --> 00:00:22.583
Lead with the bottom line.

00:00:22.583 --> 00:00:26.448
Busy readers should not have to search for the key judgment.

00:00:26.448 --> 00:00:31.015
Start with the most important assessment or finding, then explain the evidence, assumptions,

00:00:31.015 --> 00:00:32.420
confidence, implications, and limitations.

00:00:32.420 --> 00:00:34.528
This does not mean hiding nuance.

00:00:34.528 --> 00:00:38.743
It means organizing the product so the reader can understand the conclusion

00:00:38.743 --> 00:00:40.851
before digging into the supporting details.

00:00:40.851 --> 00:00:41.905
Use precise language.

00:00:41.905 --> 00:00:45.067
Avoid dramatic wording, vague claims, unsupported certainty, and filler.

00:00:45.067 --> 00:00:48.931
Say what is known, what is assessed, and what remains uncertain.

00:00:48.931 --> 00:00:52.796
If the evidence supports a narrow judgment, keep the judgment narrow.

00:00:52.796 --> 00:00:57.363
If the assessment applies only to one business unit, one environment, one timeframe,

00:00:57.363 --> 00:00:59.470
or one data source, say so.

00:00:59.470 --> 00:01:01.930
Clear boundaries make the product more useful.

00:01:01.930 --> 00:01:03.686
Evidence should support the judgment.

00:01:03.686 --> 00:01:06.848
A strong product does not list every data point,

00:01:06.848 --> 00:01:11.063
but it includes enough evidence for the reader to understand the reasoning.

00:01:11.063 --> 00:01:15.279
It may include a short timeline, key indicators, source notes, confidence statement,

00:01:15.279 --> 00:01:16.684
and alternative explanations considered.

00:01:16.684 --> 00:01:18.441
If a recommendation is included,

00:01:18.441 --> 00:01:22.305
connect it to the judgment and to the decision maker's responsibilities.

00:01:22.305 --> 00:01:25.116
Briefings require the same discipline with less space.

00:01:25.116 --> 00:01:26.170
Answer questions directly.

00:01:26.170 --> 00:01:27.575
Distinguish facts from assessments.

00:01:27.575 --> 00:01:28.980
State confidence and uncertainty.

00:01:28.980 --> 00:01:30.737
Avoid speculating beyond the evidence.

00:01:30.737 --> 00:01:32.493
If you do not know,

00:01:32.493 --> 00:01:37.060
say what is unknown and what collection or analysis could reduce the uncertainty.

00:01:37.060 --> 00:01:40.573
Professional analysts do not need to sound certain about everything.

00:01:40.573 --> 00:01:43.032
They need to be clear and useful.

00:01:43.032 --> 00:01:46.545
After the product is delivered, review the outcome when possible.

00:01:46.545 --> 00:01:49.004
Did the product answer the real question?

00:01:49.004 --> 00:01:52.166
Was the level of detail right for the audience?

00:01:52.166 --> 00:01:53.220
Were assumptions clear?

00:01:53.220 --> 00:01:54.625
Did confidence language help?

00:01:54.625 --> 00:01:57.787
Did later evidence confirm, refine, or contradict the assessment?

00:01:57.787 --> 00:02:01.300
This feedback loop is how analytic products improve over time.
